WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.199
Film Courage: How does a screenwriter know
that their screenplay is ready for market?

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:06.690
Wendy Kram, Owner of LA4Hire: That is a very
good question.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:18.590
I think the first thing is just really essential
that a writer submit it to colleagues for

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.320
feedback.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.480
And I mean I am a professional in this field.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:32.050
A lot of times even though I am not a writer,
I write treatments, I work with writers on

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:38.629
treatments and bibles and so on and at a certain
point I’ll go through multiple drafts.

00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:41.539
I have a good built-in sense of editing.

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:46.620
But at a certain point I might think this
is great and and then I’ll send it to a

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.870
trusted colleague whether it’s another executive
at a production company or a writer whose

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.920
opinion I respect and they come back and I
don’t know if they’ll whip my a** (if

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:04.140
I can say that), but they’ll come back with
feedback and go “You know the treatment

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.390
really isn’t getting interesting until page
2.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.990
So I would cut out the first page and a half.”

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:17.939
You know what?

00:01:17.939 --> 00:01:20.619
It makes it better.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:28.969
There also times where if it’s someone who’s
opinion…you need to really stay centered

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:35.549
and on point and something I definitely feel
strongly about for writers is that especially

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:41.119
in the note taking process because let’s
say a writer sets up a project with a production

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:43.930
company, studio or network and they give notes.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:51.939
One of the worst things to do is to constantly
be defending your position.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:56.659
Producers and executives will start to feel
“You know what, life’s too short.

00:01:56.659 --> 00:01:57.960
This person isn’t receptive.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.259
They’re not fun to work with.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:00.259
They’re not getting it.

00:02:00.259 --> 00:02:02.429
They love everything they’re doing.

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:03.429
You know what?

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:09.080
I’m not going to renew or we’re going
to replace the writer.”

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:15.460
The best way for a writer to be able to take
notes is to be totally open and receptive

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:26.440
and to try to hear, almost like with a second
sense of what the executive is trying to say.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:34.330
So let’s say they say (let me think of an
example)…“I don’t think a character

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.180
should say this line.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.810
They sound kind of mean right here and I don’t
feel it.”

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:45.760
And if you’re listening rather than just
defending it, you say “Oh, that wasn’t

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.980
my intention.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.060
I meant that he was being defensive and a
little bit sarcastic but he was kind of trying

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.240
to say it in a charming way.”

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.680
So then the executive would say “Okay, I
get it now, but that wasn’t coming through.”

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:06.241
So then the writer can see that their intention
wasn’t coming across so that they can go

00:03:06.241 --> 00:03:10.041
back and tweak it a bit.

00:03:10.041 --> 00:03:15.810
Again, I think it is very important to stay
true to your voice and not just roll over

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:24.610
if someone says “Okay, well we have an 85-year-old
protagonist and she has dementia.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:33.650
I’ve decided I like everything else about
this story, but let’s turn her into a 13-year-old.”

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:39.050
And believe it or not, I have heard from situations
not too far away from that.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:44.610
As a writer, I think you need to defend your
position and why it was essential to the story

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:51.130
that the character was 85 with dementia.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:57.780
That might not be the best example but it’s
basically a balance between maintaining the

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.380
integrity of your vision because you can’t
just roll over for everything because you

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.360
wind up with nothing.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:10.460
You compromise artistry and any kind of creative
vision that you have.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:17.730
In terms of a writer knowing when a project
is ready, I hear a lot of writers when I’m

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:23.130
working with them they’ll say “Well, there
is always going to be improvements to be made.”

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:32.610
I think that when you are working with people
whether it’s a strong script consultant,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:40.360
development executive, friend who’s maybe
a writer whose opinion you respect, development

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:46.080
executive, assistant to producer, assistant
to a writer/director and so on, someone who’s

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:53.640
really experienced with reading material and
knowing what Studios want, that when it gets

00:04:53.640 --> 00:05:00.160
to a point where there’s less and less notes
and the collective feeling is there might

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:06.470
be some work that needs to be done because
there always will be, but it’s good enough.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:11.590
For me the bar for what is good enough is
the character that is well-developed, that

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:18.940
is really coming through even if they are
maybe irascible or somewhat unlikable, that

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.380
we still can have empathy for that character.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.280
If they’re a villain, it’s still good
to have some empathy?

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:35.080
Not always as important but for your protagonist
there’s been a distinction between sympathy,

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:40.710
what they might do doesn’t have to be sympathetic,
but you should have a degree of empathy for

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:45.220
them.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:54.080
I think it’s when the feedback comes back
pretty consistently, that this is good and

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:01.280
you’re not just looking for validation,
you have to be really open.

